. Import channel comparison
Format	Pros	Cons
CSV export	Structured, cheap to parse, near-zero error rate, no OCR/vision cost, works for every bank that offers export	Not every bank offers it; format varies wildly (delimiters, column order, date formats) → still need a per-bank/heuristic mapper; users must know how to export
PDF statement	Universally available (every bank sends one), often the only thing users actually have on hand	Needs OCR/table extraction (Textract), layout varies by bank, multi-page, cost per page, extraction errors need a user-facing review/correction step
Screenshot (mail/photo)	Lowest friction for the user — no export/download flow, works for people who just forward statements	Least reliable: variable resolution/rotation/cropping, single page usually, higher OCR error rate, easiest to spoof/most likely to contain PII from other apps in frame
Direct bank linking (deferred)	No parsing errors, real-time, richest data	Ongoing aggregator cost (Plaid-style), compliance/security burden, deferred by you already
Recommendation: CSV first (cheapest, highest accuracy, validates the whole downstream normalize/categorize/ledger-push path with minimal engineering risk), PDF second (covers the "I only have the statement" majority case, justifies the Textract investment you've already built), screenshot last and behind a stricter confidence gate — treat it as a degraded-accuracy path that should route flagged/low-confidence rows to a manual-review queue rather than auto-posting to the Ledger. Your Gatekeeper already does format detection for all three, so the sequencing question is really about where you invest hardening effort first — I'd put it into the CSV mapper (many bank formats) before polishing screenshot OCR further.

2. Critical review of fintracker-data-pipeline
What's genuinely solid: the Step Functions choreography (Gatekeeper → Extractor → Normalizer → Dispatcher) is a clean vertical-slice implementation of the CLAUDE.md architecture, the categorizer's regex→cache→Comprehend waterfall is the right cost-control pattern, and Textract calls are wrapped with tenacity retry/backoff. Structured logging with job_id correlation is present throughout.

Multi-tenancy — this is the biggest gap. orchestrator.py:47-49 pulls user_id, statement_id, account_id from S3 object metadata/tags, not from a verified Cognito claim. Nothing in this pipeline re-validates that account_id actually belongs to user_id before transactions get pushed to the Ledger. CLAUDE.md is explicit: "Never trust a user-supplied ID from the request body for data scoping." S3 object metadata set at upload time is functionally the same trust class as a request body field — whoever generates the presigned URL/PUT is the only enforcement point, and that code isn't in this service. If that boundary is ever weaker than assumed (e.g., a shared prefix, a replayed tag, a bug in the presign endpoint), a tenant could get another tenant's transactions posted into their account. This deserves explicit verification, ideally by having the S3 processor re-derive user_id from a server-side record (e.g., look up the statement/upload record by statement_id rather than trusting the tag) instead of trusting metadata directly.

The Ledger push itself doesn't scope by tenant either. data_dispatcher/service.py:35 authenticates with a single static x-internal-api-key for all tenants — there's no X-Internal-User-Id header being forwarded, so the Ledger's UserContextFilter (per CLAUDE.md) has nothing per-request to scope against unless it's trusting whatever user_id/account_id field is embedded in the transaction JSON body itself — which is exactly the "trust the body" anti-pattern the architecture doc warns against.

Silent correctness bug: data_dispatcher/handler.py:32-33 always sets job status to COMPLETED, even when push_transactions_to_ledger reports partial or total failure (all_succeeded=False is computed and then discarded). A user whose transactions silently failed to post sees "Completed" in their UI. This should branch to FAILED (or a new PARTIALLY_COMPLETED status) when all_succeeded is false.

No failure path updates job status at all. The CDK stack (pipeline_stack.py:154-167) defines no Catch/Retry on any Step Functions task. If Textract throttles past the 3 tenacity retries, or the Normalizer throws, the execution just dies — update_job_status(..., FAILED) is never called, and the user's job sits at its last known status until the 7-day DynamoDB TTL quietly deletes it. For a SaaS with a persistent WebSocket status push, this means users can get permanently stuck jobs with no terminal notification.

Scalability/cost issues:

_load_yolo_model() in gatekeeper/service.py:29-32 reloads the model from disk on every invocation instead of caching it at module scope across warm Lambda invocations — this adds real latency/cost per statement at any volume.
Textract calls in extractor/service.py:201-219 and Ledger pushes in data_dispatcher/service.py:38-49 are both sequential loops (page-by-page, transaction-by-transaction) — no batching, no concurrency. A 20-page statement or 200-transaction push will serialize latency linearly; worth parallelizing (asyncio.gather / Step Functions Map state) once volume grows.
No per-tenant rate limiting or quota on upload/pipeline triggers — a single tenant uploading a huge or malicious PDF (e.g., hundreds of pages) can consume Lambda concurrency and Textract/Comprehend spend for everyone, since there's no cap on _pdf_to_images page count before it's handed to inference.
Security hygiene:

IAM policies in the CDK stack use resources=["*"] for both textract:AnalyzeDocument and comprehend:ClassifyDocument (pipeline_stack.py:86, :101-104) — contradicts the "least-privilege" claim in the stack's own docstring; these should scope to specific resource ARNs where the API supports it.
The Comprehend endpoint ARN in categorizer/service.py:78 has a literal * in place of an AWS account ID — this looks like unfinished/placeholder code that will fail at runtime, not a security issue but worth flagging as broken.
No idempotency key on the S3→Step-Functions trigger. S3 event notifications are at-least-once; a duplicate delivery re-runs the whole pipeline and double-posts transactions to the Ledger (no dedupe by statement_id in data_dispatcher).
Statement text (bank name, dates, balances) is extracted via regex from OCR output with no PII masking before logging — logger.info(..., bank_name=metadata.bank_name) is fine, but if any log line ever includes the full extracted text or raw transaction rows, that's account/PII data landing in CloudWatch. Worth an explicit audit of what gets logged end-to-end.
Priority order I'd fix in: (1) verify/lock down the user_id/account_id trust boundary at the S3 processor, since that's the one that turns into a cross-tenant data leak; (2) fix the COMPLETED-on-failure bug and add Step Functions Catch blocks so failed jobs actually report FAILED; (3) add idempotency on the Ledger push; (4) tighten IAM resource scoping and cache the YOLO model; (5) address concurrency/rate-limiting as volume grows.

